<h1>IP Address Middleware</h1>

<p>
    IP Address Middleware is used to capture and log the client's IP address
    for every incoming HTTP request. This middleware helps in tracking users,
    monitoring requests, security auditing, rate limiting, and request analysis.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Step 1: Create IpAddressMiddleware.cs File</h3>

<p>
    Create a new file named <strong>IpAddressMiddleware.cs</strong> inside the
    <strong>Middlewares</strong> folder.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
namespace Dotnet_Tutorials.Middlewares
{
    public class IpAddressMiddleware
    {
        private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

        public IpAddressMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
        {
            _next = next;
        }

        public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
        {
            var ipAddress = context.Connection.RemoteIpAddress;

            Console.WriteLine($"User IP Address: {ipAddress}");

            await _next(context);
        }
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 2: Register the Middleware</h3>

<p>
    Register the middleware inside <strong>Program.cs</strong>.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.UseMiddleware&lt;IpAddressMiddleware&gt;();
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Add this line before:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.UseRouting();
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>How It Works</h3>

<ul>
    <li>Middleware intercepts every incoming HTTP request.</li>
    <li>It accesses the current request using <strong>HttpContext</strong>.</li>
    <li>The client's IP address is retrieved using <strong>context.Connection.RemoteIpAddress</strong>.</li>
    <li>The IP address is printed in the console.</li>
    <li>The request is then forwarded to the next middleware using <strong>await _next(context)</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h3>Output Example</h3>

<pre>
<code>
User IP Address: ::1
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    <strong>::1</strong> represents the localhost IPv6 address when running the application locally.
</p>